  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">At a multi-stakeholder consultation on the UN Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF) in June 2021, leaders of the Thai Trade Union Congress (TTUC), the Labour Congress of Thailand (LCT), the National Congress Private Industrial of Employees (NCPE) and the State Enterprises Workers&apos; Relations Confederation (SERC), submitted and presented joint priorities and recommendations on the trade union role and engagement in the UN reform process and achievement of SDGs. Other stakeholders, including UN agencies, recognized the important role of unions and the priorities submitted. This was the first time that the role and stance of trade unions were widely recognized by the CSOs, the government, employers and UN agencies. This was followed up through a dialogue with the UN Resident Coordinator in Thailand at the end of the year.  IT is expected that these inputs will shape the UNSDCF and also the UNCT work-plans in Thailand. The ILO organized a series of webinars (regional, sub-regional and national) using the ILO publication &quot;United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation and the Decent Work Agenda &#x2013; A Trade Union Reference Manual&quot;.

The ILO assisted Thai unions to raise awareness of better/more engagement in social dialogue including all the processes of UN reforms.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.2.2. Number of workers&apos; organizations that produce proposals to be considered in social dialogue mechanisms for policymaking</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
